  "markdown": "3x4 Simultaneous ECG Report\n\nPatient Details\nName: John Sample\nID: 123123\nGender: Male\nDate of Birth: 1/25/1950 (61 years)\nHeight: 6 ft 3 in\nWeight: 223 lbs\n\nRecording Details\nRecorded: 2/17/2011 2:21:27 PM\nDevice: CL 131568\nLocation:\n\nMeasurements\nHeart Rate: 80 bpm\nP Duration: 102 ms\nPR Interval: 186 ms\nQRS Duration: 100 ms\nQT Interval: 360 ms\nQTc Interval: 416 ms\nP,QRS,T Axis: 49°, 50°, 49°\nInterpretation (Unconfirmed)\n004 Normal sinus rhythm\n193 Anteroseptal infarction, probably old\n239 Ischemic ST-T changes in posterior\nleads\n\nComments:\n\n<figure type=\"CHART\">\n\n<description>This image displays a standard 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) tracing on a red grid background. The ECG shows electrical activity of the heart across 12 leads, organized in a grid of three columns and four rows. The first three rows contain leads I, II, III (left column), aVR, aVL, aVF (middle column), and V1, V2, V3 (right column), while the fourth row shows V4, V5, V6 (right column). A long rhythm strip for lead II is displayed at the bottom. The traces show normal sinus rhythm. Text at the bottom indicates a scale of 25.0 mm/s, 10.0 mm/mV, 5mm/sqr., and filters set to 0.05 - 150 Hz.</description> <table><thead><tr><th>Lead</th><th>Tracing Characteristics</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>Standard limb lead I showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Standard limb lead II showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Standard limb lead III showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>aVR</td><td>Augmented vector right lead showing inverted P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>aVL</td><td>Augmented vector left lead showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>aVF</td><td>Augmented vector foot lead showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>V1</td><td>Precordial lead V1 showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>V2</td><td>Precordial lead V2 showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>V3</td><td>Precordial lead V3 showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>V4</td><td>Precordial lead V4 showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>V5</td><td>Precordial lead V5 showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>V6</td><td>Precordial lead V6 showing P-QRS-T complex</td></tr><tr><td>Rhythm Strip (II)</td><td>Extended rhythm strip for lead II at the bottom</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n\nPage 1 of 9 Printed: Wednesday, March 02, 2011\n\nCopyright QRS Diagnostic\n\nML122 Rev.6/11\n\n<!-- doc_id=parse-01m7rvemtapban92yj7jvfz4yn -->",
